%T A397607 349,397,401,409,421,433,461,463,499,521,523,541,547,571,577,593,601,
%U A397607 613,617,631,661,673,691,701,733,739,751,757,761,769,787,811,821,829,859,877,881,911,937,953,967,991,997
%N A397607 Bernoulli-primitive primes: p such that denominator(B_{p-1})/p is not the denominator of any Bernoulli number B_k.
%C A397607 The odd primes p for which lambda(denominator(B_{p-1})/p) = p - 1, where Carmichael lambda function is A002322.
%C A397607 The Bernoulli-primitive Carmichael numbers can be defined in the same way (required divisibility occurs).
%C A397607 Unlike Bernoulli-primitive primes, almost all Carmichael numbers are primitive in this sense, except 1105, 63973, 825265, 1909001, 2628073, ... (from _Amiram Eldar_). The last of them also does not meet the weaker condition lambda(denominator(B_{k-1})) = k - 1. Cf. A317210 (see comments). While all primes satisfy this.
%C A397607 The numbers of terms not exceeding the (10^k)-th odd prime, for k = 1, 2, ..., are 2, 31, 419, 4370, 44108, 440649, 4375856, ... . Does this sequence have a positive relative asymptotic density within the odd primes? - _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 01 2026
%H A397607 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A397607/b397607.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A397607 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A397607/a397607_1.png">Fraction of odd primes in the range [3, prime(n)] that are terms in A397607</a>.
%t A397607 q[p_] := CarmichaelLambda[Denominator[BernoulliB[p-1]]/p] == p-1; Select[Prime[Range[2, 168]], q] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Jul 31 2026 *)
%o A397607 (PARI) den(n) = if(n == 0 || (n > 1 && n % 2), 1, vecprod(select(x -> isprime(x), apply(x -> x + 1, divisors(n))))); \\ A027642
%o A397607 lambda(n) = lcm(znstar(n)[2]); \\ A002322
%o A397607 isok(p) = p > 2 && isprime(p) && lambda(den(p-1)/p) == p-1; \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Jul 31 2026
%Y A397607 Cf. A002322, A027642, A065091, A248614, A317210.
%K A397607 nonn
%O A397607 1,1
%A A397607 _Thomas Ordowski_, Jul 31 2026
